    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Mickey Moniak** leads the way as our strongest play with a 75.75 efficiency score. He draws an optimal matchup against Roki Sasaki; the combination of a +14% park factor and the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery significantly boosts his stolen base upside, a critical lever given our emphasis on scarcity. Similarly, **Isaac Paredes** (68.00) is locked into a high-leverage spot against Walbert Ure\u00f1a. While **Paredes** is traditionally a patient hitter, his platoon advantage and favorable park adjustments make him an anchor for RBI production in the middle of our order.\n\nWe are keeping a close watch on **Jose Caballero** (61.03) and **Corbin Carroll** (57.20). While **Caballero** has a high ceiling due to park factors, his matchup against Chris Bassitt is tempered by the pitcher\u2019s elite ability to hold runners, which limits his most valuable asset. **Carroll** faces Payton Tolle, a tough southpaw, forcing us to account for a platoon penalty that drags his score down despite his elite talent. The most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley** (56.58). He was the closest to cracking the lineup, but the optimizer ultimately favored the high-floor, platoon-advantaged **Vinnie Pasquantino** (58.16) for the utility slot, as **Riley** was penalized for batting 7th in the projected order.\n\nRisks remain elevated across the board as every projected spot is currently \"Lineup Pending.\" We are particularly wary of the matchups for **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll**, both of whom are facing the southpaw Tolle; should the official lineups shift these players into less favorable spots, their already precarious scores against a tough arm could dip below our 40-point bench threshold. We will continue to monitor weather patterns and confirmed batting orders to ensure our pursuit of SB efficiency does not compromise our daily game caps.",
